WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
When Jeff Bezos announced he was&nbsp;
blasting off to space in the summer,

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.560
there was a petition that he&nbsp;
shouldn’t return to Earth.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.640
Actually, there were quite a few.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.040
Questions like this often pop up online:

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.240
“Why do people like Elon&nbsp;
Musk and dislike Jeff Bezos?”

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.640
And Musk has just been named the&nbsp;
2021 Time person of the year.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.960
We’re going to examine the reasons Musk&nbsp;
has an army of fangirls and fanboys

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.080
whereas Bezos not so much, and why Musk is&nbsp;
starting to alienate some people as well.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.720
A Quora user gave his two cents on&nbsp;
why he thinks Musk is more likable:

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.400
Maybe because…He engages people&nbsp;
more via his social media.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.360
Musk loves to respond to the&nbsp;
general public on Twitter,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.400
allowing him to build a powerful connection&nbsp;
with his 65 million and counting followers.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.480
He shares memes, dad jokes, inappropriate&nbsp;
jokes - the opposite of a carefully crafted

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.280
corporate image which appeals to his fan base.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.160
When Bezos uses Twitter, he’s super professional.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.600
Like this tweet about how Amazon&nbsp;
proved its early doubters wrong.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.680
Musk poked fun at him by replying&nbsp;
with a silver medal emoji.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.280
Bezos didn’t bother responding but his rocket&nbsp;
company Blue Origin has blasted Musk’s SpaceX,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.240
labeling SpaceX’s Starship lunar&nbsp;
lander 'immensely complex &amp; high risk'.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.960
NASA didn’t think so when it awarded SpaceX&nbsp;
a contract to return American astronauts

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.600
to the moon. Blue Origin protested the&nbsp;
decision by suing and ended up losing,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.200
prompting Musk to share this meme of Judge Dredd&nbsp;
from the film saying: you have been judged.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.720
Bezos’ space venture is still&nbsp;
trying to catch up to SpaceX.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.400
Although Blue Origin has now flown&nbsp;
humans to space on New Shepard,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.600
it has never sent them into orbit around&nbsp;
Earth, something SpaceX accomplished in 2020.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.840
It has been a long and difficult road&nbsp;
to get SpaceX to where it is today.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.280
Musk has opened up about his company often to the&nbsp;
point of tears like in this 60 Minutes interview.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.160
So it’s really tough

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.480
Letting out his emotions makes&nbsp;
him appear real and relatable.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.360
His blood sweat and tears are all in the&nbsp;
name of trying to build a city on Mars.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.800
I call upon the public to support this goal.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.760
His supporters believe he’s&nbsp;
the savior of humanity.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.840
Comparing him to Tony Stark aka&nbsp;
Iron Man whereas Bezos has been

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.400
likened to Dr. Evil which possibly has&nbsp;
something to do with him being bald.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.160
It might also have something to&nbsp;
do with his riches from Amazon.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.960
Bezos grew Amazon into the behemoth it is&nbsp;
today by brilliantly giving shoppers what

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.720
they wanted when they didn’t even know&nbsp;
they wanted it, like same-day delivery.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.080
Amazon accounts for nearly 40 percent of&nbsp;
all online retail shopping in the U.S.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.640
Despite his stunning success, e-commerce&nbsp;
isn’t all that sexy so it doesn’t attract

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.360
the kind of fans you’d get if&nbsp;
you developed cool products,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.160
like what Steve Jobs did with Apple, or what&nbsp;
Musk is doing with electric cars at Tesla.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.920
Not to mention Amazon has its&nbsp;
chorus of critics accusing it of

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.720
acting like a monopoly and of mistreating workers.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.200
Headlines describe delivery drivers&nbsp;
working long days with no bathroom breaks,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.240
reportedly peeing in bottles, and warehouse&nbsp;
workers being pressured to work even faster.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.800
Bezos has defended the treatment of Amazon’s&nbsp;
staff but acknowledged the company needs to do

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.040
a better job in his final letter to shareholders&nbsp;
before he stepped down as CEO earlier in 2021.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.480
One of the reasons he stepped away&nbsp;
was to focus on his space company.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.680
Up until recently, Blue Origin&nbsp;
wasn’t all that well-known.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.720
When it IS in the spotlight,&nbsp;
Bezos has gotten flak.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.680
When he chose to rocket to space on the first&nbsp;
Blue Origin flight carrying passengers, Democratic

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.800
senator Elizabeth Warren was not too pleased about&nbsp;
what she called a “joy ride” by a billionaire.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.560
And on the following flight that took&nbsp;
Star Trek star William Shatner to space,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:45.840
he appeared to interrupt Shatner’s&nbsp;
emotional post-fight speech.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.474
Because you’re shooting up.&nbsp;
Give me a champagne bottle,

00:03:49.474 --> 00:03:54.320
come here. I want one. I want to hear&nbsp;
this. Here, you want a little of this?

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.720
Some people called out Bezos for this moment.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.360
Musk has gotten his fair share of bad press, too.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.760
Tesla has recently faced lawsuits&nbsp;
alleging sexual harassment and

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.960
racist abuse at its Fremont factory in California,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.280
and a complaint that it failed to tell the&nbsp;
public that its solar panels could catch fire.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.680
Musk has apparently responded to the&nbsp;
whistleblowing by promoting a new Tesla product,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.640
the Cyberwhistle inspired by the Cybertruck.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.440
He even tweeted: “Blow the whistle on Tesla!”

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.960
Further cementing the belief that&nbsp;
he doesn’t act like a typical CEO

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.120
which again plays to his fan base.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.560
However, the cult of Elon&nbsp;
may be showing some cracks.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.120
Especially with his tweets about the pandemic.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.680
He’s called the coronavirus panic dumb.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.640
Falsely predicted there would be no new&nbsp;
cases of covid in the US by April 2020

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.720
And defied a local lockdown order&nbsp;
to reopen Tesla’s Fremont factory.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.920
He also once called a diver who&nbsp;
refused his offer of a submarine

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.480
to save Thai boys trapped in a cave&nbsp;
a “pedo guy” in a now deleted tweet.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.000
When Musk surpassed Bezos to become&nbsp;
the richest person in the world in

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.160
early 2021 - his wealth appears&nbsp;
to have rubbed some the wrong way.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.920
Democratic senator Bernie Sanders has&nbsp;
been demanding billionaires be taxed

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.320
more to which Musk responded: I keep&nbsp;
forgetting that you’re still alive.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.640
The United Nations food program director,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.440
David Beasley called on billionaires and&nbsp;
specifically Musk to donate $6 billion,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.920
or 2% of his wealth, to help solve&nbsp;
world hunger during an interview on CNN.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.880
Wake up, smell the coffee, and help.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.360
In response, Musk said: "If WFP&nbsp;
can describe on this Twitter thread

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.560
exactly how $6B will solve world hunger, I&nbsp;
will sell Tesla stock right now and do it."

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.320
Beasley clarified that the money is needed to&nbsp;
solve the current crisis, not all world hunger.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.400
Whether you’re a billionaire, millionaire,&nbsp;
or just someone with concern for others,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.160
it can be difficult to know the best&nbsp;
way to give to have the greatest impact.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.760
That’s why the work of my&nbsp;
sponsor GiveWell is so crucial.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.280
GiveWell is a non-profit that does&nbsp;
in-depth research on the charities

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.400
that do the best job saving or improving lives.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.480
They figure out how far your dollar goes

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.400
so you can rest assured that your&nbsp;
donations are being used effectively.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.120
For example, they recommend Helen Keller&nbsp;
International’s Vitamin A Supplementation Program

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.560
that provides the key nutrient for vision, growth,&nbsp;
and immunity to children in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.880
GiveWell publishes all of their research and&nbsp;
recommendations on their website for FREE

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.880
and you don’t have to sign up to see it.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.560
If you’d like to make a difference in&nbsp;
someone’s life this holiday season,

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.840
GiveWell is matching donations from first-time&nbsp;
donors dollar for dollar up to $250.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.360
Click on the link below in my description&nbsp;
